<clickhouse>
    <!-- ClickHouse reads the host's RAM, not the container's cgroup limit, so
         without an absolute cap it sizes its caches for the whole machine and
         the container is OOM-killed. 4 GiB inside a 5 GiB limit.

         The gap between the two is not spare change: the server counts its own
         RSS against this number, and the allocator keeps pages the caches have
         already released, so idle RSS sits well above what is in use. At 2.5
         inside 3 that gap ran out and queries failed MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED with
         nothing running.

         **When the live suites start failing MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, restart
         this container before touching the number.** Idle RSS creeps toward the
         cap as the demo ingests: seen at 4.00 GiB of 4.00 GiB with
         `system.metrics.MemoryTracking` reporting 1.40 GiB, so 2.6 GiB was
         allocator-retained rather than in use, and a restart reclaimed all of
         it. There is no flag to turn, and that has now been checked against
         ClickHouse's source rather than assumed: its compiled-in jemalloc
         config is already `background_thread:true, dirty_decay_ms:5000,
         muzzy_decay_ms:0` (contrib/jemalloc-cmake/CMakeLists.txt). A MALLOC_CONF
         override was tried on 2026-09-15 and reverted — see the comment in
         `compose.infra.yaml`, which records why it made things worse. This is
         the shape of the problem, not a misconfiguration. Raising the cap moves
         the day it recurs and nothing else. -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>4294967296</max_server_memory_usage>

    <!-- Defaults are sized for a dedicated server. On a laptop sharing memory
         with Kafka, Postgres and the app services they are the largest waste. -->
    <mark_cache_size>536870912</mark_cache_size>
    <index_mark_cache_size>134217728</index_mark_cache_size>
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>

    <!-- Dropping whole parts instead of mutating rows is what makes both the
         daily TTL and the per-tenant retention job cheap. -->
    <merge_tree>
        <ttl_only_drop_parts>1</ttl_only_drop_parts>
    </merge_tree>
</clickhouse>
